{
  "id": "superposition",
  "title": "Superposition: One Source at a Time",
  "eyebrow": "linear circuits",
  "language": "en",
  "duration": 51,
  "topic": "Superposition: in a linear circuit with multiple sources, the response from each source can be computed in isolation (with others zeroed) and summed.",
  "subject_id": "ade",
  "chapter_number": 3,
  "prerequisites": [
    "kirchhoff-voltage-law",
    "kirchhoff-current-law"
  ],
  "beats": [
    {
      "id": "manimoIntro",
      "start": 0,
      "end": 6.5,
      "narration": "Two sources, one resistor — what's the current through it? Mesh equations work, but there's a slicker way.",
      "visuals": {
        "layout": "center",
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "manimo-enter",
            "content": "Manimo bobs in centre-screen",
            "delay": 0
          },
          {
            "type": "text-caption",
            "content": "Two sources, one current — solve them one at a time.",
            "delay": 0.7,
            "color": "var(--chalk-100)",
            "position": "right of Manimo, serif italic"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "setup",
      "start": 6.5,
      "end": 15.15,
      "narration": "Here's the circuit. A voltage source V one on the left, a current source I two on the right, and a load resistor R L in the middle.",
      "visuals": {
        "layout": "center",
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "svg-path",
            "content": "Battery source V1 on left",
            "delay": 0.4,
            "color": "var(--chalk-200)"
          },
          {
            "type": "svg-path",
            "content": "Current source I2 on right (circle with arrow)",
            "delay": 1,
            "color": "var(--chalk-200)"
          },
          {
            "type": "svg-path",
            "content": "Two series resistors R1, R2 framing R_L in the middle branch",
            "delay": 0.8,
            "color": "var(--amber-400)"
          },
          {
            "type": "svg-label",
            "content": "Question mark over I_L",
            "delay": 2,
            "color": "var(--rose-300)"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-caption",
            "content": "what's the current through R_L?",
            "delay": 3.8,
            "color": "var(--chalk-300)",
            "position": "below diagram"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "killV2",
      "start": 15.15,
      "end": 27.41,
      "narration": "Step one: kill the current source — replace it with an open circuit. Now only V one drives the network. Charge flows along this path, and the current through R L is the first contribution.",
      "visuals": {
        "layout": "center",
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "svg-path",
            "content": "Current source replaced by open (X mark)",
            "delay": 0.4,
            "color": "var(--rose-300)"
          },
          {
            "type": "svg-circle",
            "content": "Charge dots flow along V1-only path through R_L",
            "delay": 1,
            "color": "var(--amber-400)"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-formula",
            "content": "I_L^(1) = V1 / (R1 + R_L)",
            "delay": 4,
            "color": "var(--amber-300)",
            "position": "below diagram"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "killV1",
      "start": 27.41,
      "end": 39.68,
      "narration": "Step two: bring back the current source, but kill V one — replace it with a short. Now I two drives the network. Charge flows the other way through R L; that's the second contribution.",
      "visuals": {
        "layout": "center",
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "svg-path",
            "content": "V1 replaced by wire (short)",
            "delay": 0.4,
            "color": "var(--rose-300)"
          },
          {
            "type": "svg-circle",
            "content": "Charge dots flow from I2 path through R_L (opposite direction)",
            "delay": 1,
            "color": "var(--teal-400)"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-formula",
            "content": "I_L^(2) = I2 · R1 / (R1 + R_L)",
            "delay": 4,
            "color": "var(--teal-400)",
            "position": "below diagram"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "sum",
      "start": 39.68,
      "end": 51,
      "narration": "Add the two contributions and you've found the current with both sources live. Superposition works because the circuit is linear — sources add their effects independently.",
      "visuals": {
        "layout": "center",
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "text-formula",
            "content": "I_L = I_L^(1) + I_L^(2)",
            "delay": 0.4,
            "color": "var(--amber-300)",
            "position": "centre, large serif"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-caption",
            "content": "works for ANY linear circuit — no matter how many sources",
            "delay": 1.8,
            "color": "var(--chalk-200)",
            "position": "below formula"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-caption",
            "content": "(kill V → short, kill I → open — and watch the signs)",
            "delay": 3.4,
            "color": "var(--chalk-300)",
            "position": "small mono caption"
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
}
